1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,261
I've been doing some research into what people in Britain think of doctors, the

2
00:00:05,261 --> 00:00:07,390
ones who work in general practice -

3
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:12,393
the first call for medical care - and comparing this with the situation in

4
00:00:12,393 --> 00:00:13,900
a couple of other countries.

5
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:18,650
I want to talk about the rationale behind what I decided to do.

6
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:23,986
Now I had to set up my programme of research in three different countries

7
00:00:23,986 --> 00:00:28,780
so I approached postgraduates in my field in overseas departments,

8
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:33,560
contacting them by email, to organize things for me at their end.

9
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:39,480
I thought I would have trouble recruiting help but in fact everyone was very willing

10
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:42,490
and sometimes their tutors got involved too.

11
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:49,135
I had to give my helpers clear instructions about what kind of sample population

12
00:00:49,135 --> 00:00:50,500
I wanted them to use.

13
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:57,063
I decided that people under 18 should be excluded because most of them are students

14
00:00:57,063 --> 00:00:59,130
or looking for their first job,

15
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:04,656
and also I decided at this stage just to focus on men who were

16
00:01:04,656 --> 00:01:05,440
in employment,

17
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:08,672
and set up something for people who didn't have jobs

18
00:01:08,672 --> 00:01:13,210
and for employed women later on as a separate investigation.

19
00:01:13,210 --> 00:01:16,667
I specifically wanted to do a questionnaire,

20
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:19,420
and interviews with a focus group.

21
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:25,549
With the questionnaire, rather than limiting it to one specific point, I wanted to

22
00:01:25,549 --> 00:01:27,900
include as much variety as possible.

23
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:32,457
I know questionnaires are a very controlled way to do things

24
00:01:32,457 --> 00:01:37,878
but I thought I could do taped interviews later on to counteract the effects

25
00:01:37,878 --> 00:01:38,320
of this.

26
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:44,744
And the focus group may also prove useful in future, by targeting subjects I

27
00:01:44,744 --> 00:01:49,120
can easily return to, as the participants tend to be more involved.

28
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:52,920
So I'm just collating the results now.

29
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:57,622
At the moment it looks as if, in the UK, despite the fact that

30
00:01:57,622 --> 00:02:01,640
newspapers continually report that people are unhappy with medical care,

31
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:05,194
in fact it is mainly the third level of care,

32
00:02:05,194 --> 00:02:07,463
which takes place in hospitals,

33
00:02:07,463 --> 00:02:09,090
that they are worried about.

34
00:02:09,090 --> 00:02:15,388
Government reforms have been proposed at all levels and although their success is not

35
00:02:15,388 --> 00:02:21,365
guaranteed, long-term hospital care is in fact probably less of an issue than the

36
00:02:21,365 --> 00:02:22,930
media would have us believe.

37
00:02:22,930 --> 00:02:27,060
However, I've still got quite a bit of data to look at.

38
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:33,864
Certainly I will need to do more far-reaching research than I had anticipated in

39
00:02:33,864 --> 00:02:38,560
order to establish if people want extra medical staff invested in the community,

40
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:41,822
or if they want care to revert to fewer,

41
00:02:41,822 --> 00:02:44,500
but larger, key medical units.

42
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:51,285
The solution may well be something that can be easily implemented by those responsible

43
00:02:51,285 --> 00:02:55,360
in local government, with central government support of course.

44
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:59,500
This first stage has proved very valuable though.

45
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:04,034
I was surprised by how willing most of the subjects were to get involved

46
00:03:04,034 --> 00:03:09,170
in the project - I had expected some unwillingness to answer questions honestly.

47
00:03:09,170 --> 00:03:14,945
But I was taken aback and rather concerned that something I thought I'd set

48
00:03:14,945 --> 00:03:20,360
up very well didn't necessarily seem that way to everyone in my own department.

49
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:29,934
I thought you might also be interested in some of the problems I encountered

50
00:03:29,934 --> 00:03:31,500
in collecting my data.

51
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:36,637
There were odd cases that threw me - one of the subjects who I

52
00:03:36,637 --> 00:03:41,293
had approached while he was out shopping in town, decided to pull out when

53
00:03:41,293 --> 00:03:43,500
it came to the second round.

54
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:47,791
It was a shame as it was someone who I would like to have

55
00:03:47,791 --> 00:03:49,000
interviewed more closely.

56
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:56,113
And one of the first-year students I interviewed wanted reassurance that no names would

57
00:03:56,113 --> 00:03:57,740
be traceable from the answers.

58
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:59,848
I was so surprised,

59
00:03:59,848 --> 00:04:05,048
because they think nothing of telling you about themselves and their opinions in seminar

60
00:04:05,048 --> 00:04:05,590
groups!

61
00:04:05,590 --> 00:04:10,120
Then, one of the people that I work with got a bit funny.

62
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:16,625
The questions were quite personal and one minute he said he'd do it, then

63
00:04:16,625 --> 00:04:17,970
the next day he wouldn't,

64
00:04:17,970 --> 00:04:20,660
and in the end he did do it.

65
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:26,505
It's hard not to get angry in that situation but I tried to keep

66
00:04:26,505 --> 00:04:30,000
focused on the overall picture in order to stay calm.

67
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,113
The most bizarre case was a telephone interview I did with a teacher at

68
00:04:37,113 --> 00:04:38,660
a university in France.

69
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:44,238
He answered all my questions in great detail- but then when I asked how

70
00:04:44,238 --> 00:04:50,077
much access he had to dangerous substances he wouldn't tell me exactly what his

71
00:04:50,077 --> 00:04:51,120
work involved.

72
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:53,400
It's a real eye-opener...
